+There is something to digest. Based on our discussions about a more advanced knowledge graph, I've come up with this: The self-evolving knowledge graph is a dynamic architectural pattern that shifts the burden of data modeling from human architects to a collaborative loop between Large Language Models and formal logic. Unlike a traditional database that requires a predefined schema, this system begins with a "Seed Ontology"—a skeleton of essential classes like **Legal Entity** or **Agent**. As users interact with the system, an LLM parses natural language into atomic units of meaning known as logical triples, which represent specific facts or relationships found within the conversation.
+
+The core of this evolution lies in the "Discovery Phase," where extracted triples are compared against the existing world model. When the LLM identifies a relationship or category it hasn't seen before, it doesn't simply discard it as an error. Instead, it treats the new term as a "Schema Candidate." For instance, if the system knows about "Organizations" but the LLM extracts a fact about a "Limited Liability Partnership," the architecture recognizes a gap in its hierarchy and prepares to expand the ontology to accommodate this new sub-type.
+
+To maintain integrity and prevent the "Frigga-ma-jiggits" problem—where nonsense or slang enters the formal record—the system employs a validation gatekeeper. This layer uses the "Baseline Constraints" of the ontology to ensure that new facts don't violate fundamental logic. A reasoner checks that the subject and object of a triple are compatible with the proposed relationship. If a triple is structurally valid but semantically weird, it is relegated to a "Staging Graph" rather than being instantly integrated into the core long-term memory.
+
+As the system matures, it performs "Ontology Reconciliation" to keep the graph clean and lean. Using techniques like semantic similarity or vector embeddings, a maintenance agent reviews the staging area to merge synonyms. If the LLM has extracted triples for "Corp," "Corporation," and "Inc," the reconciliation engine identifies that these all refer to the same concept within the **Legal Entity** hierarchy. It then "morphs" the ontology by collapsing these into a single canonical class, ensuring the graph stays navigable and computationally efficient.
+
+This "GenDev" approach creates a "Threshold of Truth" based on evidence and frequency. A single mention of a new term might remain volatile, but if a concept appears consistently across multiple conversation turns, the system automatically triggers an "Insertion" event. This updates the underlying OWL file, effectively allowing the machine to "learn" the specific jargon and organizational structures of your firm, Collective Intelligence, without you ever having to manually write a line of XML or RDF.
+
+Ultimately, the self-evolving knowledge graph serves as the "Long-Term Memory" for agentic frameworks like **Friday**. By storing knowledge in a machine-readable OWL format rather than just a flat vector store, the system retains the ability to perform complex reasoning and "ISA" pathfinding. This ensures that as your business evolves and your transition plans progress, the AI isn't just reciting text—it is navigating a structured, ever-growing map of your professional reality that remains logically consistent over time.
