+this is the next iteration of the PRD for the SEKG. Product Requirements Document: Self-Evolving Knowledge Graph (SEKG)Project: Friday Agent FrameworkModule: friday-memoryVersion: 2.1Status: Approved for Design1. Executive SummaryThe Self-Evolving Knowledge Graph (SEKG) is the long-term semantic memory engine for the Friday agent. It addresses the critical limitations of vector-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) by enforcing a structured, logical representation of information.Unlike static knowledge graphs that require manual ontology engineering, the SEKG is designed to be autopoietic (self-creating). It ingests unstructured conversation data, extracts logical triples (Subject-Predicate-Object), and automatically "morphs" its underlying schema based on the frequency and stability of new information. This allows the Friday agent to learn domain-specific jargon, organizational hierarchies, and complex relationships without explicit human programming, providing a deterministic "Source of Truth" that evolves alongside the user.2. Problem StatementContext Drift in RAG: Vector similarity searches frequently return irrelevant results because they lack a structural understanding of relationships (e.g., failing to distinguish between "Friday the Assistant" and "Friday the day of the week").Schema Rigidity: Traditional ontologies (OWL/RDF) are brittle. They require predefined classes and properties, making them unsuitable for dynamic domains where terminology shifts rapidly.Lack of Deep Reasoning: Current LLM assistants cannot reliably answer multi-hop questions (e.g., "Who owns the company that developed the project I worked on last week?") because this requires traversing a graph, not just matching keywords.3. User StoriesIDActorStoryAcceptance CriteriaUS.1SystemAs the Extraction Engine, I want to decompose user prompts into atomic triples (Subject-Predicate-Object) automatically.Input: "Chuck created Friday."  Output: (Chuck, created, Friday)  Must handle zero-shot extraction without pre-training.US.2SystemAs the Gatekeeper, I want to validate new triples against a set of baseline logic constraints before storing them.If created has Domain: Person and Range: Product, then (Rock, created, Friday) must be flagged or rejected if "Rock" is not a Person.US.3SystemAs the Morphing Engine, I want to detect when a new relationship type appears frequently and promote it to the Core Ontology.If predicate:delegated_to appears > 5 times with consistent usage, it is automatically added as a valid ObjectProperty in the OWL schema.US.4AssistantAs the Reasoning Layer, I want to traverse the graph to answer questions about indirect relationships.Query: "Who works for the company Chuck founded?"  Logic: Find Chuck -> founded -> Company -> employs -> Employee.US.5SystemAs the Reconciliation Agent, I want to merge synonym nodes to prevent knowledge fragmentation.The system must identify that Corp. and Corporation refer to the same class and merge them into a single canonical node.US.6UserAs a User, I want the system to ask for clarification when a new fact is ambiguous.If the user says "Friday is slow," the system asks: "Do you mean the software framework or the day of the week?"US.7DeveloperAs a Maintainer, I want to visualize the graph's evolution over time.A visual export (e.g., DOT/GraphViz) showing which nodes/edges were added in the last session.4. Logical ArchitectureThe SEKG is composed of four distinct processing engines that function in a continuous loop.4.1. Universal Extraction Engine ("The Triplicator")Responsibility: Converts unstructured text into structured graph data.Logic:Ingests raw text from the Friday conversation loop.Performs Zero-Shot Triple Extraction to identify Entities and Relations.Tags every extraction with a confidence score and a timestamp.Output: A stream of "Candidate Triples" sent to the Gatekeeper.4.2. Structural Gatekeeper ("The Validator")Responsibility: Ensures the logical integrity of the graph.Logic:Checks Candidate Triples against the Seed Ontology (Core classes: Entity, Action, Context).Enforces Domain & Range Constraints (e.g., An Action cannot be the subject of another Action).Output:Valid: Passed to Staging.Invalid: Rejected.Ambiguous: Triggers a clarification prompt to the User (US.6).4.3. Morphing & Discovery Engine ("The Evolution Layer")Responsibility: Manages the expansion of the schema.Logic:Maintains a Staging Graph (Short-term memory) and a Core Ontology (Long-term memory).Evidence Counter: Tracks the frequency of new predicates/classes in the Staging Graph.Promotion Threshold: A configurable parameter (default: $N=5$ occurrences). When a candidate meets the threshold, it is formalized into the Core Ontology.Rollback: Logic to demote a class if it remains unused for a set period.4.4. Semantic Reconciliation Agent ("The Janitor")Responsibility: Deduplication and graph hygiene.Logic:Runs as an asynchronous background task.Uses Vector Embeddings to calculate cosine similarity between node labels.Identifies candidates for merging (e.g., "Software Engineer" vs. "Developer").Executes graph rewriting to unify edges under canonical nodes.5. Integration with Friday FrameworkThe SEKG will be implemented as a standalone Python package: friday.memory.Interface:friday.memory.ingest(text: str): Entry point for the extraction loop.friday.memory.query(sparql: str): Interface for the Assistant to retrieve knowledge.friday.memory.reason(question: str): Higher-level method that converts natural language to graph queries.Storage:Filesystem: Local .owl (Ontology) and .sqlite (Triples) for single-user deployments.In-Memory: NetworkX graph for rapid reasoning during active sessions.6. Opportunities, Risks, and DifficultiesOpportunitiesDomain Agnosticism: The system starts generic but becomes a specialist (Legal, IT, Medical) purely through interaction.Explainability: Unlike black-box embeddings, every answer derived from the SEKG can be traced back to a specific triple and source interaction.RisksSemantic Pollution: If the Gatekeeper is too loose, the graph will fill with noise (slang, typos), degrading retrieval quality.Performance Latency: Real-time graph reasoning is computationally expensive. Validation must occur <200ms to maintain conversational flow.Infinite Loops: Poorly defined transitive properties could cause the reasoner to hang.Difficulty AssessmentOverall Complexity: High (8/10).Primary Challenge: The Morphing Engine. Algorithmic determination of when to "harden" a temporary fact into a permanent ontology class is a non-trivial research problem.7. Success MetricsRecall Precision: >90% accuracy in retrieving specific entity attributes (e.g., "What is Chuck's role?") compared to <60% for standard RAG.Schema Convergence: The rate of new schema creation should decay over time as the system "learns" the domain, stabilizing after ~500 interaction turns.Deduplication Rate: The Reconciliation Agent should reduce total node count by >30% compared to raw extraction.

